// Open product questions, answered in the running app:
//
//     cargo run -- --review            (this file; answers -> answers.ron)
//
// Schema: `gradiance_scene::review` (QuestionSet). Process: docs/orchestration.md,
// "Review mode". Option paths are relative to this file. Prefer `.scm` scenes:
// a `.ron` scene is rejected by the next FORMAT_VERSION bump, a script is not.
// Each option's scripts run in order, one frame apart, so a later script can
// index (`body-index-at`) what an earlier one spawned.
//
// A question with `decision: true` is a plan decision: its options are text
// (a caption, no scene). Use it where no scene can show the difference.
(
    version: 1,
    questions: [
        (
            id: "order-4b-1",
            title: "When does brief 4b-1 (group op surface) run?",
            source: "docs/state.md Position (4b-1 'in flight'); unified-surface-plan.md sec. 10 rows 1 and 5",
            description: r#"docs/state.md says 4b-1 is in flight and runs first. On 2026-11-17 GitHub had no branch and no pull request for it, and D-0 ran before it.

4b-1 adds `set-color` and the group verbs. The plan then has U-2 move those verbs onto the operation registry. If 4b-1 has not started, the verbs can be written once, on the registry, after U-2."#,
            decision: true,
            options: [
                (
                    label: "After U-2, on the registry",
                    caption: r#"Recommended if 4b-1 has not started. The verbs are written once as registry operations. Cost: review scenes keep their re-rolled colours until then (no `set-color`). 4b-2 already waits for U-2, so nothing else moves."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "Now, before D-1",
                    caption: r#"The plan as written. 4b-1 lands on today's hand-written verb path and U-2 migrates it. `set-color` arrives sooner; the group verbs are written twice."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "It is in work elsewhere",
                    caption: r#"A session or a local branch already holds 4b-1. Leave the order alone. Say in the note where the work is, so later sessions do not start a second copy."#,
                ),
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "op-output",
            title: "What does an operation return when it lowers?",
            source: "unified-surface-plan.md sec. 13.1 (PR #239); brief U-1",
            description: r#"Each operation has one `lower` step that every doorway (Lisp, BRP/MCP, CLI, UI) calls. The first plan gave it the return type 'one intent'. The catalogue has 61 operations: 25 edit, 25 query, 2 config, 9 editor. An intent fits the 25 edits only.

This decides the trait that U-2 then applies to all 61, so a wrong shape is found late."#,
            decision: true,
            options: [
                (
                    label: "Messages + a value",
                    caption: r#"Recommended. `lower` returns `OpOutput`: zero or more messages for their existing queues, plus an optional return value. One trait covers all four categories. U-1 proves it with five operations: three edits, one query (`body.count`), one config (`sim.set`). U-1 grows by two operations."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "An intent only",
                    caption: r#"The first plan. U-1 stays at three edit operations and is smaller. Queries, config and editor operations keep their hand-written verbs until U-2 decides their shape; the conformance tests cover edits only until then."#,
                ),
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "op-context",
            title: "Where does an operation read old values from?",
            source: "unified-surface-plan.md sec. 13.2 (PR #239); brief U-1",
            description: r#"An edit needs the old value to be undoable (`place` needs the old pose). Scripts get it today from `SceneView`, a copy of the scene taken once per run, because a Steel builtin cannot hold `&World`.

The first plan moved that snapshot into the new bottom crate `gradiance-op`. It cannot go there: it holds `ShapeDef` and `BodyPhysics`, which live in crates above."#,
            decision: true,
            options: [
                (
                    label: "Mirrors in a context",
                    caption: r#"Recommended. `gradiance-op` defines `OpContext`, a container of read-only mirrors, and names none of them. `gradiance-command` owns the scene snapshot; `ui` owns `PanelStates`. Every doorway fills the context with one builder. Cost: the snapshot grows when an operation needs a new old value, as it does today."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "Read the World directly",
                    caption: r#"`lower(params, &World)`. No snapshot to keep current. But the Lisp doorway must then queue its calls and lower them after the script ends, and query verbs still need the snapshot to answer at their call site. Two read paths exist, and a bad argument is reported at the end of the run, not at its line."#,
                ),
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "op-decoder",
            title: "How many decoders turn a call into typed parameters?",
            source: "unified-surface-plan.md sec. 13.3 (PR #239); brief U-1",
            description: r#"A Lisp call arrives as positional values; a BRP, MCP or CLI call arrives as named JSON. Both must become the same typed parameter struct. Each extra decoder is a place where two doorways can disagree about the same call."#,
            decision: true,
            options: [
                (
                    label: "serde decodes, reflection describes",
                    caption: r#"Recommended. Every doorway builds a named JSON object and calls one serde decoder. Lisp adds only a value conversion and the positional-to-named mapping. Reflection gives the schema, the docs and completion. One test checks that the schema and the decoder agree."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "Reflection does both",
                    caption: r#"One description of the parameters, and no serde derive on them. Not verified: Bevy's reflection deserializer needs every nested type registered, and its handling of defaults and of the unit newtypes is untested here."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "Two decoders and a test",
                    caption: r#"The first plan: reflection decodes Lisp, serde decodes JSON, and a conformance test compares them. More code, and the test becomes the only guard."#,
                ),
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "op-keys",
            title: "How do key bindings join the operation registry?",
            source: "unified-surface-plan.md sec. 13.4 (PR #239); owner ruling 'keys are out of scope' (2026-11-17)",
            description: r#"The bindings do not change in either option. The question is how much of the input layer U-2 rebuilds. Today `EditorAction` (a leafwing enum, 14 use sites) maps chords to actions, and the handlers write intents directly."#,
            decision: true,
            options: [
                (
                    label: "Link table, keep the enum",
                    caption: r#"Recommended. One table maps each `EditorAction` variant to an operation id; the handler invokes that id; a test checks the table. The enum and the leafwing `InputMap` stay. A generated key map waits for a brief that needs user-editable keys."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "Generate the key map",
                    caption: r#"The first plan. U-2 removes `EditorAction` and builds the `InputMap` from each operation's `key`. Keys then have one source, and user overrides get a place. It is a rebuild of the input layer with no visible result while keys are out of scope."#,
                ),
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "toolchain-file",
            title: "Keep `rust-toolchain.toml` next to CI's `RUST_PIN`?",
            source: "brief D-0 Approach step 3 (PR #240)",
            description: r#"D-0 moved the compiler to rustc 1.100.0. The brief left one choice open: add a `rust-toolchain.toml`, or keep the version only in `ci.yml`. PR #240 adds the file. This also selects the compiler on your own machine: `rustup` installs 1.100.0 the first time `cargo` runs in the repository."#,
            decision: true,
            options: [
                (
                    label: "Keep the file",
                    caption: r#"Recommended; PR #240 as it stands. Local, agent and CI builds use one compiler without anyone reading `ci.yml` (agent containers still default to 1.97.0). A test asserts that the file and `RUST_PIN` are equal. The `coupling` job and `release.yml` then also build with the pinned version, not with the newest stable."#,
                ),
                (
                    label: "CI pin only",
                    caption: r#"Remove the file from PR #240. Your machine keeps whatever toolchain it has. Each agent session must install and select 1.100.0 itself, or it gates on an older clippy than CI."#,
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
